    <statement><p>Find an interval of t-values such that <m>c(t) = (\cos{t}, \sin{t})</m> traces the lower half of the unit circle (in the counter-clockwise direction).</p>
<p>interval = <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>Note: Use lowercase "pi" for <m>\pi</m>.</p>
<p>Example answer: <m>\left[0,1\right]</m>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left[3.14159,6.28319\right]</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>t = \pi</m>, we have <m>c(\pi) = (-1, 0)</m>.  As t increases from <m>\pi</m> to <m>2\pi</m>, the x-coordinate of <m>c(t)</m> increases from -1 to 1, and the y-coordinate decreases from 0 to -1 (at <m>t = \frac{3\pi}{2}</m>) and then returns to 0.  Thus, for t in <m>[3.14159,6.28319]</m>, the equation traces the lower part of the circle.


</solution>
